INT. PROVINCIAL RAILWAY WAITING ROOM - NIGHT

A potbellied stove. A clock that has been wrong for years. Two benches. One lamp. SIMONE DUVALIER (60s, widow's black, one suitcase) sits stiffly. HENRIK ASTRUP (60s, overcoat, cane) sits on the other bench, not looking at her.

SIMONE
You have been staring at that clock for an hour.

HENRIK
It's wrong.

SIMONE
It has been wrong since the war.

HENRIK
Then why don't they fix it?

SIMONE
Perhaps they prefer it that way. Perhaps everyone who passes through here prefers one clock that lies.

HENRIK
Madame, do I know you?

SIMONE
No.

HENRIK
You look at me as though I should.

SIMONE
You are mistaken. I was looking at your cane.

HENRIK
It was my father's.

SIMONE
It is a handsome cane.

HENRIK
It was the only thing he left me. That and a quarrel I never finished.

SIMONE
With whom?

HENRIK
With him. He died before I could win it.

SIMONE
(a small laugh)
Those are the ones that last. The quarrels with the dead.

HENRIK
You speak as one who knows.

SIMONE
I buried a husband in March. We were not speaking when he fell ill. I thought there would be a better time.

HENRIK
There never is.

SIMONE
No.

HENRIK
Where do you travel?

SIMONE
To my sister's. She lives at the end of the line. She has not spoken to me in twenty years, and she has written to say she is dying.

HENRIK
And you will go?

SIMONE
I have a ticket.

HENRIK
That is not an answer.

SIMONE
It is the only one I have tonight.

HENRIK
(after a moment)
My train comes at seven. I am going to my son. He has not spoken to me in nine years.

SIMONE
Then we are both late.

HENRIK
It would seem so.

SIMONE
Do you know what I think, Monsieur Astrup?

HENRIK
You know my name.

SIMONE
It is on your suitcase. I have read it eleven times.

HENRIK
(smiling for the first time)
Then you may as well call me Henrik.

SIMONE
Simone.

HENRIK
Simone. What do you think?

SIMONE
I think the clock is not wrong at all. I think it shows the hour when each of us should have left.

HENRIK
And what hour is it?

SIMONE
For you, nine years ago. For me, twenty.

HENRIK
Then let us be early for the next one.

A whistle in the distance. Both rise. He offers his arm. She takes it.
